    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d29">

                <pb facs="0160" n="28" sameAs="frus:frus1926v01/pg28"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.C114/584</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Assistant Chief of the Division of Western European Affairs</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Richardson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-11-08"><hi rendition="#italic">November 8,
                        1926</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Richardson</hi>: I have read the attached
                    memorandum on the World Court.<anchor n="36" xml:id="d29-f1"/> As I remember, I
                    looked it up last year with Senator Pepper, Mr. Lenroot and others and, under
                    the Resolution of the Senate, we cannot sign the Protocol until every nation
                    signatory to the original Statute of the World Court has accepted the
                    reservations. It does not make any difference whether they ratified it by their
                    Congress, Senate or other body. There is no use of communicating with the
                    governments that have accepted. Every one of them must accept or we cannot join
                    and the President will in no way recommend any change in the situation.</p>
                <closer><signed>F[<hi rendition="#small-caps">rank</hi>] B. K[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ellogg</hi>]</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
